1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,707
Good morning.

2
00:00:01,707 --> 00:00:06,907
Now, we’ve been asked to choose an aspect of European clothing or fashion

3
00:00:06,907 --> 00:00:10,360
and to talk about its development over time.

4
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:15,200
I decided to focus on a rather small area of clothing

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,960
and that’s pockets.

6
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:20,090
I chose pockets for two reasons, really.

7
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:24,262
We all have them – in jeans, jackets, coats, for example –

8
00:00:24,262 --> 00:00:30,102
and even though we often carry bags or briefcases as well, nothing is quite

9
00:00:30,102 --> 00:00:35,943
as convenient as being able to pop your phone or credit card into your

10
00:00:35,943 --> 00:00:36,360
pocket.

11
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:42,882
Yet, I suspect that, other than that, people don’t really think about pockets too much

12
00:00:42,882 --> 00:00:46,550
and they’re rather overlooked as a fashion item.

13
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:53,135
It’s certainly very interesting to go back in time and see how pockets developed for men

14
00:00:53,135 --> 00:00:53,910
and women.

15
00:00:53,910 --> 00:00:59,634
In the 18th century, fashions were quite different from the way they are now,

16
00:00:59,634 --> 00:01:01,270
and pockets were too.

17
00:01:01,270 --> 00:01:08,160
If we think about male fashion first … that was the time when suits

18
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:09,220
became popular.

19
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:15,690
Trousers were knee-length only and referred to as ‘breeches’, the waistcoats were short

20
00:01:15,690 --> 00:01:18,178
and the jackets were long,

21
00:01:18,178 --> 00:01:25,146
but all three garments were lined with material and pockets were sewn into this

22
00:01:25,146 --> 00:01:28,630
cloth by whichever tailor the customer used.

23
00:01:28,630 --> 00:01:34,842
The wearer could then carry small objects such as pencils or coins on their person

24
00:01:34,842 --> 00:01:38,570
and reach them through a gap in the lining.

25
00:01:38,570 --> 00:01:45,260
Coat pockets became increasingly decorative on the outside for men who wanted to look

26
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:45,738
stylish,

27
00:01:45,738 --> 00:01:52,428
but they were often larger but plainer if the wearer was someone with a

28
00:01:52,428 --> 00:01:58,640
profession who needed to carry medical instruments – a doctor or physician, for example.

29
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:04,280
The development of women’s pockets was a little different.

30
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:09,751
For one thing, they weren’t nearly as visible or as easy to reach as

31
00:02:09,751 --> 00:02:10,480
men’s.

32
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:16,292
In the 18th and 19th centuries, women carried numerous possessions on their person

33
00:02:16,292 --> 00:02:21,210
and some of these could be worth a lot of money.

34
00:02:21,210 --> 00:02:29,293
Women were more vulnerable to theft and wealthy women, in particular, worried constantly about

35
00:02:29,293 --> 00:02:29,870
pickpockets.

36
00:02:29,870 --> 00:02:34,875
So – what they did was to have a pair of pockets made that

37
00:02:34,875 --> 00:02:36,800
were tied together with string.

38
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:39,682
The pockets were made of fabric,

39
00:02:39,682 --> 00:02:44,966
which might be recycled cloth if the wearer had little money

40
00:02:44,966 --> 00:02:50,730
or something more expensive, such as linen, sometimes featuring very delicate embroidery.

41
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:56,930
Women tied the pockets around their waist so that they hung beneath their clothes.

42
00:02:56,930 --> 00:03:02,215
Remember, skirts were long then and there was plenty of room to hide a

43
00:03:02,215 --> 00:03:07,500
whole range of small possessions between the layers of petticoats that were commonly worn.

44
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:12,928
They would have an opening in the folds of their skirts through which they

45
00:03:12,928 --> 00:03:16,030
could reach whatever they needed, like their perfume.

46
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:23,411
Working women, of course, also needed to carry around items that they might use for whatever job

47
00:03:23,411 --> 00:03:26,016
or trade they were involved in,

48
00:03:26,016 --> 00:03:33,397
but their pairs of pockets still remained on the inside of their clothing, they just got bigger

49
00:03:33,397 --> 00:03:36,870
or longer – sometimes reaching down to their knees!

50
00:03:36,870 --> 00:03:41,834
So the tie-on pockets went well into the 19th century

51
00:03:41,834 --> 00:03:47,790
and only changed when fashion altered towards the end of that period.

52
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:51,629
That’s when dresses became tighter and less bulky,

53
00:03:51,629 --> 00:03:57,174
and the pairs of pockets became very noticeable – they stood out too much

54
00:03:57,174 --> 00:04:00,160
and detracted from the woman’s image.

55
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:05,635
Women who had been used to carrying around a range of personal possessions –

56
00:04:05,635 --> 00:04:11,110
and still wanted to – needed somewhere to carry these items about their person.

57
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:13,620
That was when small bags,

58
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:20,649
or pouches as they were known, came into fashion and, of course, they inevitably

59
00:04:20,649 --> 00:04:27,678
led on to the handbag of more modern times, particularly when fashion removed pockets

60
00:04:27,678 --> 00:04:28,180
altogether.
